{#
  Heading: Dual display heading with the alt-glyph device.

  A glyph swap is an explicit per-headline judgment, never automatic. The
  letterform has to reinforce what that specific word means. Zero swaps is
  always fine, and swapping for symmetry or because a letter happens to have an
  alternate is forbidden.

  The glyphSwaps filter (eleventy.config.js) enforces the rules structurally:
  a hard cap of two, h1/h2 only, and the swapped letter must fall inside the
  named word. Violations warn at build time and render plain.

  `swaps` takes one object or an array of at most two:
    swaps={ word: "searching", letter: "e" }
    swaps=[{ word: "found", letter: "o" }, { word: "built", letter: "u" }]

  Optional per-swap `flavor` picks the stylistic set (alt-soft, alt-bar,
  alt-dot, alt-cut, alt-stencil), defaulting to the `alt` combo as Heading.jsx
  does. Which flavor suits a given swap is still pending sign-off in design.md
  section 8 item 5, so leaving it unset is the safe choice today.

  Usage:
    {% raw %}{% from "components/heading.njk" import heading %}
    {{ heading("Your customers are searching.", as="h1",
               swaps={ word: "searching", letter: "e" }) }}
    {{ heading("What's included", as="h3") }}{% endraw %}
#}
{% macro heading(text, as="h2", swaps=none, class="", id="") %}
<{{ as }}{% if id %} id="{{ id }}"{% endif %}{% if class %} class="{{ class }}"{% endif %}>{{ text | glyphSwaps(swaps, as) | safe }}</{{ as }}>
{% endmacro %}
